{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-236696",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+d,\\,f(1)=___\\,,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1-6(1)=-5 \\\\ f(2) & =1-6(2)=-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =f(2)-f(1) \\\\  & =-11-(-5) \\\\  & =-11+5 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $f(1)=-5$ and the common difference is $d=-6.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)-6,\\,f(1)=-5,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
